PHASE 2 -- rl, photons=100, 29 specs, alpha=0.1, 5 seeds

1. Does the conclusion depend on the error metric?

   shift axis                    coverage (1-SSIM)    coverage (rel l2)   n
   none (in-distribution)           0.891+/-0.011        0.927+/-0.006    1392
   operator shift (Zernike)         0.604+/-0.017        0.754+/-0.024    384
   operator shift (physical)        0.915+/-0.015        0.956+/-0.009    576
   content shift                    0.839+/-0.010        0.969+/-0.002    3480
   combined                         0.708+/-0.016        0.912+/-0.005    960

   largest coverage difference between metrics: 0.204
   The two metrics are different functions of the reconstruction, so
   exact agreement is not expected. What matters is whether the
   ordering and the qualitative failure survive the swap.

   Spearman rho(1-SSIM, rel l2) on the test split: +0.734

2. Do the physically-motivated families behave like the Zernike ladder?

   family        kind      rung       |mismatch|    error  cov(SSIM)  cov(l2)   n
   defocus       zernike   extreme        0.9774   0.6024      0.396    0.604   48
   astigmatism   zernike   extreme        0.8916   0.5540      0.417    0.646   48
   coma          zernike   extreme        0.9679   0.5915      0.333    0.625   48
   spherical     zernike   extreme        0.9242   0.5552      0.875    0.750   48
   na            physical  p2             0.0768   0.1454      0.896    0.979   48
   wavelength    physical  p2             0.0478   0.1631      0.896    0.979   48
   focus_drift   physical  p4             0.3938   0.2563      0.771    0.896   48

   Zernike  families: coverage 0.333-0.875, |mismatch| 0.8916-0.9774
   physical families: coverage 0.771-0.896, |mismatch| 0.0478-0.3938

   Read these together with the magnitude column. A physical family
   that retains coverage while producing a much smaller kernel
   perturbation has not disproved the mechanism -- it has placed
   realistic parameter uncertainty lower on the same axis.

